{
 "id": "eliyahu-hanavi",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": true,
 "title": "Eliyahu HaNavi",
 "description": "Eliyahu HaNavi (\"Elijah the Prophet\"), the old Hebrew refrain sung at the close of the Sabbath, which A. Z. Idelsohn, writing in 1929, called the oldest part of the Elijah songs that Jewish communities already sang there in the eleventh century, here to the Eastern Ashkenazi folk tune he printed, in four parts of our own. It calls on Elijah three times: the prophet, the Tishbite, the Gileadite. Sung three times through, the third time softly.",
 "language": "he",
 "difficulty": "beginner",
 "genre": [
  "jewish",
  "shabbat",
  "traditional"
 ],
 "style": "straight",
 "tempo": 88,
 "key": "Gm",
 "timeSignature": "3/4",
 "pickupBeats": 0,
 "credits": {
  "words": "traditional Hebrew refrain",
  "tune": "traditional Eastern Ashkenazi folk tune (as printed by A. Z. Idelsohn, 1929)",
  "arrangement": "Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: a traditional Hebrew refrain, the oldest part of the Elijah songs sung at the close of the Sabbath, which Idelsohn (1929, pp. 360 to 361) traces to the eleventh century",
  "tune": "public domain: traditional, printed by A. Z. Idelsohn in 1929 (he died in 1938)",
  "arrangement": "original, written for Stackapella",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "melody and words from A. Z. Idelsohn, Jewish Music in its Historical Development (New York: Henry Holt and Company, copyright 1929), p. 401, Table XXXII, 'Tunes of the Eastern Ashkenazic folk-song', No. 1, Moderato, in E minor, 3/4, the tune alone, no harmony (his text on p. 392 calls it one of two very popular tunes for 'Eliyohu Hannovi'; Idelsohn died in 1938); written here in G minor, a minor third up, so the tune (B3 to C5 as printed, D4 to E-flat 5 here) sits in the soprano's range. Only the metrical first section, up to the print's Fine ('Eliyohu hanovi, eliyohu hatishbi, eliyohu, eliyohu, eliyohu hagilodi'), sung three times; the print's free recitative after it ('bimheroh yovo elenu', recitando, D.C. al Fine) is left out, as is the longer second half sung today, which this print doesn't have. The words are the print's, respelled from its Ashkenazi transliteration in modern Israeli pronunciation ('E-li-ya-hu ha-na-vi', 'ha-tish-bi', 'ha-gil-a-di'), syllable for syllable as the notes carry them; the print's sixteenth notes on 'li' and 'a' are sung by the lower parts on their held chord. Breaths written in for beginners: the half notes of 'navi', 'tishbi' and 'giladi' (except the last) each give up three quarters of a beat. Read from the scan at archive.org/details/jewishmusicinits00idel_0 (leaf n418), a 1944 printing by the Tudor Publishing Company, New York, of the 1929 book, with Holt's 1929 copyright page."
 },
